 cyst decompression (aspiration and sclerosis), laparoscopic/surgical fenestration, denervation, nephrectomy. Evaluation/testing of at-risk relatives permits early diagnosis and management.\n\n3) Abdominal decompression in normal pregnancy. Hofmeyr GJ, Kulier R. Cochrane Database Syst Rev. 2000;(2):CD001062. PMID: 10796233. (Updated) Cochrane Database Syst Rev. 2012 Jun 13;(6):CD001062. PMID: 22696321. Background: Abdominal decompression was developed for pain relief during labour; also used for pregnancy complications and attempted to improve fetal wellbeing and intellectual development.\n\n4) Abdominal decompression for suspected fetal compromise/pre-eclampsia. Hofmeyr GJ. Cochrane Database Syst Rev. 2000;(2):CD000004. PMID: 10796079. (Updated) Cochrane Database Syst Rev. 2012 Jun 13;(6):CD000004. PMID: 22696313. Background: Same historical context\u2014used for fetal compromise and pre-eclampsia management; Cochrane reviews available (2000, updated 2012).\n\n5) Abdominal decompression during pregnancy. Br Med J. 1968 May 11;2(5601):317-8. PMID: 5648966. (No abstract available)\n\n6) Cardiorespiratory transition in CDH. Sankaran D, Lakshminrusimha S, Lim MJ. Semin Fetal Neonatal Med. 2025 Oct;30(3):101649. Epub 2025 Jul 1. PMID: 40615338. Review: Congenital diaphragmatic hernia (CDH) = incomplete diaphragm closure with herniation of abdominal contents into chest; fetal hemodynamics altered due to pulmonary/alveolar and vascular hypoplasia.\n\n7) Abdominal decompression in the treatment of the toxaemias of pregnancy. Blecher JA, Heyns OS. Lancet. 1967 Sep 23;2(7517):621-4. PMID: 4166789. (No abstract available)\n\n8) Abdominal compartment syndrome in a monochorionic-triamniotic triplet pregnancy complicated by feto-fetal transfusion syndrome. Obata K et al. J Obstet Gynaecol Res. 2021 Sep;47(9):3370-3373. Epub 2021 Jul 7. PMID: 34235810. Case: 40-year-old primigravida with MT triplets hospitalized at 16 gestational weeks; polyhydramnios in two fetuses and oligohydramnios in third \u2192 diagnosed feto-fetal transfusion syndrome; reported abdominal compartment syndrome.\n\nOther page/navigation details\n- \u201cClipboard, Search History, and several other advanced features are temporarily unavailable\u201d (page note).\n- PubMed site is an official U.S. government (.gov) site; connection via https.\n- Send/Save options available: Clipboard, My Bibliography, Collections, Citation manager, Email, Create RSS.\n- Page includes links to NCBI/NCBI Literature Resources (MeSH, PMC, Bookshelf) and NLM contact/address.\n\nIf you want, I can extract the full list of all 111 PMIDs or export the page-1 citations into a .nbib or other citation format.",
