  "content": "Title/ citation\n- Hofmeyr GJ. Abdominal decompression for suspected fetal compromise/pre-eclampsia. Cochrane Database Syst Rev. 2012. Search date: Cochrane Pregnancy and Childbirth Group's Trials Register, 2 February 2012.\n\nObjective\n- Assess effects of antenatal abdominal decompression (versus no decompression) for maternal hypertension and/or impaired fetal growth on perinatal outcomes.\n\nStudy selection and methods\n- Included randomized or quasi-randomized trials in women with pre-eclampsia and/or suspected fetal compromise.\n- Eligibility and trial quality assessed by one review author.\n- Three studies included; all potentially subject to serious bias.\n\nIncluded studies (references)\n- Blecher JA. MD thesis, 1967.\n- MacRae DJ, Mohamedally SM, Willmott MP. J Obstet Gynaecol Br Commonw 1971;78:636\u201341.\n- Varma TR, Curzen P. J Obstet Gynaecol Br Commonw 1973;80:1086\u201394.\n(One study excluded: Coppola 1985.)\n\nMain results (therapeutic abdominal decompression vs no decompression)\n- Persistent pre-eclampsia: RR 0.36 (95% CI 0.18 to 0.72).\n- Fetal distress in labour: RR 0.37 (95% CI 0.19 to 0.71).\n- Low birthweight: RR 0.50 (95% CI 0.40 to 0.63).\n- Apgar score <6 at 1 minute: RR 0.26 (95% CI 0.12 to 0.56).\n- Perinatal mortality: RR 0.39 (95% CI 0.22 to 0.71).\n- Other outcomes assessed in included trials: induction of labour (all indications), induction for placental insufficiency, urinary oestriol increase (mg/week), fetal biparietal diameter increase (mm/week), birthweight (detailed above).\n\nAuthors\u2019 conclusions\n- Because of methodological limitations and risk of bias in the included trials, effects of therapeutic abdominal decompression are not clear.\n- Apparent improvements in birthweight and perinatal mortality warrant further evaluation of abdominal decompression for impaired fetal growth and possibly for women with pre-eclampsia.\n\nOther\n- Conflict of interest: none known.\n- Earlier related review: Hofmeyr GJ. Cochrane Database Syst Rev. 2000;(2):CD000004.",
