2026-07-26 02:14:45,996 - sciconharness.mcp_client.utils.utils - INFO - Full Results:
{
  "url": "https://www.bmj.com/content/bmj/2/5913/238.full.pdf",
  "title": "238.full.pdf",
  "content": "Summary of content (BMJ, first published 4 May 1974; DOI 10.1136/bmj.2.5913.238)\n\n1) Abdominal decompression in pregnancy\n- Origin and claims\n  - Introduced by O. S. Heyns (1959). Claimed benefits when applied intermittently in labour and during last 12 weeks of pregnancy: shortened labour and pain relief; prevention/treatment of pre-eclampsia; prevention/treatment of fetal asphyxia; improved fetal mental/physical outcomes.\n- Evidence and methodological issues\n  - Early studies lacked randomized, blinded controls; apparatus obvious to patients; selection bias (patients mainly social classes I\u2013II).\n  - Isotope studies (refs 5,6) showed transient increased counts over placental site during decompression, but sustained effect over pregnancy is unproven.\n  - Claims of increased pregnanediol and oestriol excretion cited (ref 7).\n- Larger randomized double-blind trial (Coxon et al., 1973; ref 8)\n  - Design: All patients received decompression; randomized to -70 mm Hg (n=200) versus -20 mm Hg (n=211) assuming -20 mm Hg as ineffective control.\n  - Outcomes:\n    - Mean placental weight significantly higher in the -20 mm Hg (control) group.\n    - Mean birth weight higher in control group (not statistically significant).\n    - Stillbirths: 1 in control (short cord) vs 3 in high-decompression group (1 anencephaly, 2 unexplained).\n    - Admission rates for pre-eclampsia nearly identical; high-decompression group had significantly shorter hospital stay when admitted for pre-eclampsia.\n    - Overall conclusion: abdominal decompression did not reduce blood pressure in pregnancy nor significantly influence pregnancy/labour characteristics, baby size or maturity.\n  - Limitations: unexpectedly low pre-eclampsia admission rates; possible self-selection of low-risk patients.\n- Smaller study in \u201csmall-for-dates\u201d fetuses (Varma & Curzen, 1973; ref 9)\n  - N=140 pregnancies; decompression daily 30 minutes using \u221280 to \u221290 mm Hg for 25 seconds each minute.\n  - Results:\n    - More inductions for placental insufficiency in control group: 27 vs 20.\n    - More forceps deliveries for fetal distress in control group.\n    - Biparietal diameter growth: treated 2.08 mm/week vs control 1.49 mm/week (P<0.001).\n    - Greater urinary oestriol increase and significantly heavier babies in treated group (P<0.001).\n    - Authors concluded a beneficial effect on small-for-dates fetuses.\n  - Limitations: not blinded; 7 patients crossed from decompression to control after randomization.\n- Proposed mechanisms\n  - Directly increased intervillous blood flow via negative pressure.\n  - Indirectly improved intervillous drainage by displacing uterus away from inferior vena cava (achievable by posture; ref 10).\n- Editorial conclusion\n  - Large double-blind trial with negative findings (Coxon et al.) makes widespread clinical adoption unlikely, though some smaller trials report benefits; overall evidence remains conflicted.\n\nKey references (selected)\n- Heyns OS. J Obstet Gynaecol Br Empir 1959;66:220.\n- Coxon A, Fairweather DVI, Smyth CN, Frankenberg J, Vessey M. J Obstet Gynaecol Br Commonw 1973;89:1081.\n- Varma TR, Curzen P. J Obstet Gynaecol Br Commonw 1973;80:0186.\n- Humphrey M et al. J Obstet Gynaecol Br Commonw 1973;80:1075.\n- MacRae DJ et al. J Obstet Gynaecol Br Commonw 1971;78:636.\n\n2) Methoxyflurane nephrotoxicity\n- Pharmacology and clinical use\n  - Blood:gas solubility ratio \u2248 13.0 (slow induction and recovery); high lipid solubility permits use of low concentrations; non-irritant to respiratory tract.\n  - Used clinically; limited uptake in the UK; used in low concentrations for analgesia in midwifery.\n- Evidence of renal toxicity\n  - Early report suggested methoxyflurane might cause renal damage.\n  - Mazze et al. (1971) found methoxyflurane alone can cause polyuric renal insufficiency.\n  - Cousins & Mazze (1973; ref 3) demonstrated renal damage occurs when exposure exceeds ~2.0 MAC-hours (MAC \u00d7 duration).\n  - Animal studies: additive nephrotoxicity when combined with gentamicin or tetracyclines.\n- Metabolism and mechanism\n  - Methoxyflurane metabolizes to inorganic fluoride and oxalic acid.\n  - Inorganic fluoride implicated: inhibits enzyme systems; chronic fluoride ingestion causes renal damage; proposed mechanism\u2014fluoride inhibition of sodium-pumping enzymes in the ascending loop of Henle/early distal tubule.\n  - Oxalic acid ruled out as principal cause based on differing pathology and clinical signs.\n- Clinical implications\n  - High concentrations for \u22651 hour increase renal damage risk even with normal baseline renal function.\n  - Low concentrations used for analgesia in midwifery have not yet been shown harmful, but concentration and duration should be monitored carefully.\n\nKey references (selected)\n- Crandell WB et al. Anesthesiology 1966;27:591.\n- Mazze RI, Shue GL, Jackson SH. JAMA 1971;216:278.\n- Cousins MJ, Mazze RI. JAMA 1973;225:1611.\n- Mazze RI, Cousins MJ. Br J Anaesth 1973;45:394.\n\n3) Gonorrhoea of the pharynx (Bro-J\u00f8rgensen & Jensen series)\n- Study population and methods\n  - Routine pharyngeal cultures performed in Copenhagen after an initial April 1970 case of asymptomatic pharyngeal gonococci following orogenital contact.\n  - Total tested: ~2,700 persons (including 804 men and 542 women with gonorrhoea, and similar numbers without gonorrhoea).\n- Findings\n  - Neisseria gonorrhoeae cultured from the pharynx in 110 patients: 55 women, 43 heterosexual men, 12 homosexual men.\n  - Pharynx was the only infected site in 8 women, 6 heterosexual men, 2 homosexual men.\n  - Among patients with gonorrhoea, pharyngeal infection rates:\n    - Women: 10%\n    - Heterosexual Danish men: 7%\n    - Heterosexual foreign men: 2%\n    - Homosexual men: 25%\n- Sexual behaviour data (consecutive series of 1,203 heterosexual Danish patients: 523 women, 680 men)\n  - Orogenital contact frequency: ~20% never, ~75% frequently/fairly often, ~35% at most recent contact.\n  - Orogenital contact nearly universal among homosexual men (consistent with higher pharyngeal infection rate).\n- Clinical presentation\n  - Pharyngeal gonorrhoea usually asymptomatic: 79% symptom-free.\n  - Symptomatic cases had sore throat with tonsillitis, occasionally exudates.\n\nKey reference\n- Bro-J\u00f8rgensen and Jensen; study initiated April 1970 (publication details within the article series).",
