2026-07-26 02:15:11,217 - sciconharness.mcp_client.utils.utils - INFO - Full Results:
{
  "url": "https://www.bmj.com/content/2/5601/317.full.pdf",
  "title": "317.full.pdf",
  "content": "Source: BMJ Leading Articles, 11 May 1968.\n\n1) Sickness absence in refinery workers\n- Three groups studied: \"frequent\" absentees, \"long\" absentees, and \"never\" absentees.\n- Major difference: attitudes toward self and work.\n  - \"Frequent\" and \"long\" sick more often recalled unhappy childhoods; \"frequent\" sick reported job dislike or frustrated ambition.\n  - \"Never\" sick denied personal problems and illness; yet 25% had organic disease on physical exam.\n- Eysenck personality inventory:\n  - \"Frequent\" sick: higher extraversion and neuroticism than controls.\n  - \"Never\" sick: introversion and stability.\n  - \"Long\" sick: introversion and neuroticism.\n- Methodological note: control group validly comparable only with the \"frequent\" sick group; some conclusions need confirmation.\n- Conclusions: younger men who develop frequent short sickness absences tend to continue this pattern into middle/older age, raising overall sickness rates. If confirmed, interventions could target attitudes linked to clinically flimsy sickness absence.\n\n2) Abdominal decompression during pregnancy (O. S. Heyns et al.)\n- Background: 1954 Heyns found curarization reduced labour pain; developed abdominal decompression suit to reduce abdominal wall pressure on uterus.\n- Device/method:\n  - Suit from axillae to feet with rigid spacer over lower chest/abdomen.\n  - Mechanical suction reduces abdominal wall pressure by 50\u2013150 mm Hg; abdomen protrudes, diaphragm descends.\n  - Uterus shape becomes more spherical; contractions become more isotonic (claimed more efficient).\n- Heyns' claims:\n  - 98% of patients decompressed during first stage of labour are helped (shorter labour, less pain).\n  - From 1965 recommended decompression once or twice daily from 28 weeks, often with supplemental oxygen in late pregnancy.\n  - Claimed improved maternal and fetal placental blood flow and fetal brain oxygenation leading to better intelligence and development.\n- Evidence and critiques:\n  - Mathews & Loeffler (1968): fetal scalp blood samples in early labour showed no significant acid-base or oxygen-saturation differences between decompressed and control groups; fetal blood sampling may lack sensitivity for subtle changes.\n  - Liddicoat (S. Afr. med. J., 1968): randomized follow-up of 329 infants (mothers randomly assigned to decompression or no decompression; some controls had physiotherapy). Tests at 1, 4, 9 months (South African child development scale) and at 3 years (Merrill-Palmer) showed no IQ difference between decompression and physiotherapy groups. Any apparent advantage in Heyns' reports likely due to selection bias (volunteer mothers of higher socioeconomic status/IQ), observer bias (use of Gesell tests), and reliance on maternal reports.\n  - Blecher & Heyns (Lancet, 1967): reported decompression of value in treating pre-eclampsia, but not for hypertension or chronic renal disease in pregnancy.\n- Conclusion: claims that antenatal abdominal decompression improves child intelligence or definitive fetal physiologic benefits are not proven. Authors call for rigorous experimental evaluation before widespread adoption.\n\nKey references: Heyns (1959, 1962, 1963, 1965); Heyns, Samson & Graham (Lancet 1962); Mathews & Loeffler (J Obstet Gynaec Brit Cwlth, 1968, 75:268); Liddicoat (S. Afr. med. J., 1968, 42:203); Blecher & Heyns (Lancet, 1967, 2:621).\n\n3) EEG signs of death\n- Forensic/clinical context:\n  - Distinction often made between somatic death and \"molecular\" death; somatic death pronouncement legally implies imminent death of all body cells.\n  - Traditional standard: assume cerebral death after apparent cessation of cardio-respiratory function for about five minutes.\n- Need for cerebral-function data increased by resuscitation advances and organ transplantation demand.\n- Research developments:\n  - Negovskii: proposed cerebral vs somatic death distinction.\n  - Schwab et al. (1963): proposed EEG-based criteria for cerebral death.\n  - Hockaday et al. (1965): reported accurate EEG-based predictions of fatal vs non-fatal outcomes after acute cerebral anoxia following cardiac/respiratory arrest.\n  - Prior & Volavka: attempted refinements (in press at time of article).\n  - Pampiglione: studied quality as well as survival in resuscitated children (Acta Neurochir, 1964).\n- UK situation and recommendations:\n  - Little British work due partly to shortage of trained clinical neurophysiologists.\n  - Urgent need for multidisciplinary, multi-centre studies (cardiology + neurology + intensive care).\n  - EEG advantages: objective index when clinical signs are uninformative and most sensitive available measure of cerebral-function change.\n  - Technical demands: investigations require experienced clinical neurophysiologists to avoid premature conclusions of cerebral death.\n- Key references: Schwab, Potts & Bonazzi (Electroenceph. clin. Neurophysiol., 1963, 15:147); Hockaday et al. (Electroenceph. clin. Neurophysiol., 1965, 18:575); Royal College of Physicians reports on EEG (1962, 1966); Pampiglione (1964).\n\n4) Defining the ideal ambulance\n- Criticism: current ambulances criticized for being designed without regard to effects of illness/injury\u2014particularly poor suspension and loading/unloading difficulties.\n- Industry constraint: motor industry organized for mass production; ambulances are typically converted commercial vehicles and inherit parent-design limitations; small specialist manufacturers disappearing.\n- Proposed actions:\n  - P. S. London (Birmingham Accident Hospital) recommended forming a standing committee on ambulance design following the projected formation of the Central Ambulance Services Council.\n  - Goal: develop a specification acceptable to the 136 authorities that currently purchase ambulances and equipment in the UK, enabling a targeted engineering programme.\n- Reference: British Medical Journal correspondence and presentation to automobile engineers (citation: Brit. med. J., 1967).",
  "description": "",
