**1968 BMJ editorial:**
- Heyns developed abdominal decompression suit; claims of benefit in labour, pre-eclampsia prevention/treatment, fetal oxygenation.
- Mathews & Loeffler (1968): fetal scalp blood sampling showed no acid-base/oxygenation differences.
- Liddicoat (1968): randomized follow-up of 329 infants — no IQ difference.
- Blecher & Heyns (Lancet 1967): reported decompression valuable in treating pre-eclampsia, but not for hypertension or chronic renal disease in pregnancy.

**1974 BMJ editorial:**
- Coxon et al. 1973 (J Obstet Gynaecol Br Commonw; Oxford trial): randomized double-blind trial, -70 mm Hg (n=200) vs -20 mm Hg (n=211). Outcomes: mean placental weight significantly higher in control group; mean birthweight higher in control (NS); stillbirths 1 vs 3; pre-eclampsia admissions similar; shorter hospital stay for pre-eclampsia in high-decompression group. Overall: decompression did not reduce BP or significantly influence pregnancy/labour characteristics, baby size or maturity.
- Varma & Curzen 1973: N=140 small-for-dates pregnancies; treated group had fewer inductions for placental insufficiency (20 vs 27), fewer forceps for fetal distress, faster BPD growth (2.08 vs 1.49 mm/week, P<0.001), greater oestriol increase, heavier babies (P<0.001). Limitations: not blinded; 7 crossovers.

The Cochrane review (which I can't cite as evidence per the task) pooled three trials (Blecher 1967, MacRae 1971, Varma & Curzen 1973) and found apparent benefits (RR<1 for low birthweight, perinatal mortality, etc.) but serious risk of bias. Interestingly, the Cochrane review did NOT include Coxon 1973 — wait, actually I recall the Cochrane review "Abdominal decompression for suspected fetal compromise/pre-eclampsia" included Blecher 1967, MacRae 1971, Varma 1973. The Coxon trial might have been excluded because the "control" received low-level decompression (both groups got decompression) or because it was in unselected/normal population? Actually the Coxon trial was in unselected women... Hmm, actually it might be in the "normal pregnancy" review. Either way, I won't cite the Cochrane review.

Wait — there's an important discrepancy in the 1974 BMJ editorial reference list: "Coxon A, Fairweather DVI, Smyth CN, Frankenberg J, Vessey M. J Obstet Gynaecol Br Commonw 1973;89:1081" — volume should be 80. The correct citation: Coxon A, Fairweather DVI, Smyth CN, Frankenberg J, Vessey M. "A randomised double-blind controlled trial of abdominal decompression in the last trimester of pregnancy" or similar, J Obstet Gynaecol Br Commonw 1973;80:1081-1085? Let me verify via PubMed to get the exact title and PMID.

Also verify Mathews & Loeffler 1968 (J Obstet Gynaec Brit Cwlth 75:268) and Liddicoat 1968 (S Afr Med J 42:203). These are relevant: Mathews & Loeffler relates to labour/fetal oxygenation (less relevant to antenatal hypertension/FGR). Liddicoat is about child development (normal pregnancy). For our question (hypertension or impaired fetal growth), the key trials are Blecher & Heyns 1967, MacRae 1971, Varma & Curzen 1973, and the negative Coxon 1973 trial (though in unselected population, it included pre-eclampsia outcomes).

Let me search PubMed for the Coxon trial to get exact citation.

Also, the MacRae 1971 trial details: "Clinical and endocrinological aspects of dysmaturity and the use of intermittent abdominal decompression in pregnancy." The Cochrane pooled results show benefits. From the 1974 editorial, MacRae et al. claimed increased pregnanediol and oestriol excretion.

I should also confirm details of Blecher & Heyns 1967 results. The 1968 editorial says it reported decompression of value in treating pre-eclampsia but not for hypertension or chronic renal disease. Maybe I can find more details. Perhaps not necessary.

One more relevant modern physiological reference: Jayasundara 2023 PLoS One on maternal intra-abdominal pressure and hypertension — provides context that raised IAP is associated with hypertension in pregnancy (rationale). Not essential.

Let me now verify the Coxon 1973 citation and check whether there's a Humphrey et al. 1973 paper (J Obstet Gynaecol Br Commonw 80:1075) — possibly an isotope placental blood flow study. Let me search PubMed for "Coxon abdominal decompression" and "Humphrey abdominal decompression placental blood flow".
